/// An array stored into a declared `array` property, which is a WIDENING rather than a store.
///
/// `private array $items = [];` followed by `$this->items = [1, 2, 3];` is as ordinary as PHP
/// gets, and it was refused: a declared `array` property is `array<mixed>` — boxed cells — while
/// the literal is `array<int>`, eight-byte slots. Two layouts, so the assignment is a copy, and
/// the copy already existed for the same pair at a call boundary.
///
/// The FRESH array the conversion answers is also why the store must not incref it: it is
/// already the one reference the slot takes, and the ordinary container path's retain would have
/// left one array behind per assignment.
